From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ludovic Henry <git@ludovic.dev>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 21:40:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tests: let the environment raise the run_python_subprocess()
 timeout

Upstream-Status: To upstream [no riscv64 runner upstream to regression-test it on]

run_python_subprocess() hands every child interpreter a wall-clock limit its
callers hard-code (test_tile_block_dim_mismatch, test_tile_print and
test_tile_shared_memory all pass timeout=60). The child imports warp and
JIT-compiles its kernels with Clang before it can do what the test checks, and
on the riscv64 runner that alone exceeds 60s: all three cases fail with
subprocess.TimeoutExpired at exactly the limit, while the rest of the 4151
tests pass. The same shape as the limits patches 0004 and 0005 already make
overridable, and those only cover unittest_parallel and test_tile_oob.

Read a floor for the limit from WARP_TEST_SUBPROCESS_TIMEOUT. The environment can
only raise it, and with the variable unset nothing changes for anyone.
---
 warp/tests/unittest_utils.py | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/warp/tests/unittest_utils.py b/warp/tests/unittest_utils.py
index 1a989c2..798af21 100644
--- a/warp/tests/unittest_utils.py
+++ b/warp/tests/unittest_utils.py
@@ -434,6 +434,10 @@ def run_python_subprocess(
     elif os.name == "posix":
         source = f"import resource as _resource; _resource.setrlimit(_resource.RLIMIT_CORE, (0, 0));\n{source}"
 
+    # How long the child needs to import Warp and JIT-compile its kernels with Clang is a property
+    # of the machine running the tests, so let the environment raise (never lower) the limit.
+    timeout = max(timeout, float(os.environ.get("WARP_TEST_SUBPROCESS_TIMEOUT", 0)))
+
     try:
         return subprocess.run(
             [sys.executable, "-u", "-c", source, *args],
